# Keep upstream's text files mergeable; only the fork's own binary art is
# declared binary (task 9.2).
#
# Why so little here: a merge strategy or a blanket `* binary` rule would
# silently discard upstream edits, which is the opposite of what this fork
# wants (it wants upstream changes to land and to conflict loudly when they
# touch a seam). Marking a file `binary` only tells git not to attempt a
# textual merge on it, so it is applied *only* to assets the fork alone owns
# and edits as artwork. Anything else stays default.

# --- fork-owned binary assets (single-side customisation) --------------------
channel/web/static/login-bg.jpg binary
channel/web/static/rongda-ai-mark.svg binary
desktop/src/renderer/src/assets/rongda-ai-mark.svg binary
docs/images/rongda-ai-logo.svg binary
docs/images/rongda-ai-logo-dark.svg binary
webhelp/assets/img/rongda-ai-logo.svg binary
webhelp/assets/img/rongda-ai-logo-dark.svg binary
# --- forensic evidence (byte-preserved, never mergeable) ----------------------
# An OpenSpec change may archive a source snapshot next to a SHA-256 manifest
# (see openspec/changes/harden-windows-desktop-reliability/evidence/). EOL
# normalisation rewrites those bytes, so a recorded hash would only match on the
# platform that wrote it: with autocrlf=true a Windows checkout restores CRLF,
# while Linux/macOS checkouts keep LF and every hash mismatches. `-text` stores
# the bytes verbatim, so the manifest verifies anywhere and the snapshot stays a
# faithful copy.
openspec/changes/*/evidence/** -text
